    "reading_text": "\\nIn the late 19th century, a young man named Charles Darwin set out on an expedition aboard HMS Beagle. The voyage took him around the globe and provided him with valuable insights into natural selection that would later become foundational for his groundbreaking work, 'On the Origin of Species.'\\nDuring his travels, Darwin encountered a wide variety of flora and fauna. He was particularly struck by the diversity of life on Earth and the adaptations that allowed organisms to survive in their respective ecosystems.\\nOne instance stands out: Darwin's observation of finches on the Galapagos Islands. He noticed that different species had beaks adapted for gathering various types of food, such as seeds or insects. This led him to hypothesize that natural selection could explain the evolution of species over time.",
